---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Butterfly Valve Actuators | Pneumatic Automation DN50–DN1200 | PNEUMACTUATOR"
  description="Pneumatic actuators for butterfly valve automation: rack and pinion and scotch yoke packages for DN50–DN1200 valves, concentric and double eccentric designs, ISO 5211 mounting with NAMUR accessories. Factory-direct from Wenzhou, China."
>
  <section class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-night-green">INDUSTRIES · BUTTERFLY VALVES</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight">Butterfly Valve Automation with Pneumatic Actuators</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-lg text-slate-300">Quarter-turn pneumatic actuation for DN50 to DN1200 butterfly valves in water, HVAC and process lines — sized for the torque profile of your valve and mounted direct to ISO 5211.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
      <!-- 应用背景 -->
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">APPLICATION BACKGROUND</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Why Butterfly Valves Are Automated with Pneumatic Actuators</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">A butterfly valve is a quarter-turn valve: the disc rotates a quarter turn from fully open to fully closed. The design is light, compact and inexpensive compared with other valve types, and the straight-through flow path keeps pressure drop low. Those qualities make butterfly valves common in water distribution, HVAC, irrigation and general process duty, and pneumatic actuators are the usual way to automate them where valves sit outdoors, in pits, or in positions that are awkward for operators to reach.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The torque required to turn the disc is not constant through the stroke. A resilient-seated butterfly valve develops high torque shortly after the disc leaves the seat and again as it re-enters the seat, while dynamic torque from flow typically peaks in the mid-travel region, roughly between 70° and 80° of rotation depending on the valve and flow conditions. Seat material (EPDM, NBR, PTFE or metal), disc eccentricity, line pressure and flow velocity all shift these numbers, which is why the valve manufacturer's torque curve is the starting point for any actuator selection.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Disc eccentricity matters too. Concentric, resilient-seated valves are the most common and lowest cost, but the disc rubs against the seat through much of the stroke. Double-eccentric designs lift the disc away from the seat early in travel, reducing seat friction and wear, and triple-offset designs add a metal seat for higher pressure and temperature services. Each construction presents a different torque profile to the actuator, so sizing should be checked against the valve's documented breakaway and running torque plus a working margin — 25-30% is a common industry starting point, though the right margin depends on the application, the control scheme and the duty cycle.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Selection also depends on how the valve is used. An on-off isolation valve on a water main may cycle a few times a day, while a control valve on a process line may position continuously, and the actuator and accessories differ accordingly: solenoid and limit switches for on-off, positioner and feedback for modulating. Supply air pressure is part of the sizing equation because actuator output torque is rated at a reference pressure — commonly around 5.5-8 bar in plant air systems, but confirm the actual pressure at your site before finalizing the model. Where valves are part of an emergency shutdown scheme, spring return fail-safe becomes a requirement rather than an option, and the spring torque should be verified against the valve at its worst expected operating condition.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- 推荐产品表 -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">RECOMMENDED ACTUATORS</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Actuator Families for Butterfly Valve Duty</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-6 py-3.5 font-semibold">Actuator family</th>
                <th class="px-6 py-3.5 font-semibold">Typical butterfly valve duty</th>
                <th class="px-6 py-3.5 font-semibold">When it fits</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/rack-and-pinion/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Rack and pinion</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">DN50–DN300 resilient-seated valves, on-off and modulating</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Low and mid torque band; compact, fast cycling, direct ISO 5211 mount</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50/60">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/scotch-yoke/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Scotch yoke</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">DN400–DN1200 and double/triple eccentric valves</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">High torque at the stroke ends, where butterfly valves often need it; economical in large sizes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/spring-return/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Spring return</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Any size where the valve must close (or open) on air loss</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Fail-safe position for isolation and safety duties</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">The PNEUMACTUATOR catalog covers rack and pinion, scotch yoke and spring return actuators from 8 Nm up to 200,000 Nm output torque. Confirm the torque your valve needs at your operating conditions before selecting a model.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- 关键考虑点 -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">KEY CONSIDERATIONS</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">What to Check When Automating a Butterfly Valve</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Torque margin:</span> compare actuator output at your plant air pressure against the valve's breakaway and running torque, and add a working margin — 25-30% is a common starting point; verify for your service.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Seat material:</span> EPDM, NBR, PTFE or metal seats must suit the media and temperature, and each changes the torque the actuator must deliver.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Eccentricity:</span> concentric, double-eccentric and triple-offset valves have different torque profiles and seat wear behavior.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Supply air:</span> dry, filtered air at stable pressure; actuator output torque scales with air pressure.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Fail-safe:</span> spring return to close or open when air is lost, if the duty demands it.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Control mode:</span> solenoid valve and limit switches for on-off duty; a positioner with feedback for modulating flow control.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Mounting:</span> ISO 5211 interface with NAMUR accessory pattern for direct, bracket-free installation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">FAQ</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Butterfly Valve Actuation Questions</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950">How do I size a pneumatic actuator for a butterfly valve?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Start from the valve manufacturer's torque data at your operating pressure and temperature, cover both breakaway and running torque, then add a working margin. Compare that against the actuator's output torque at your supply air pressure across the full travel — not just at a single point — and confirm the ISO 5211 interface size matches the valve top flange.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950">What changes for the actuator with double-eccentric or triple-offset valves?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Eccentric designs move the disc away from the seat early in the stroke, which lowers seat friction and breakaway torque and extends seat life on frequent cycling. The torque profile differs from a concentric valve, so sizing should follow the valve-specific curve. These valves are often specified at higher pressures and temperatures, which can push the choice toward a scotch yoke.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Can the same actuator run both on-off and modulating duty?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The actuator itself can, but the accessories differ. On-off duty uses a solenoid valve with limit switches for position confirmation. Modulating duty needs a positioner and continuous feedback, and the actuator should be selected for frequent positioning cycles — a rack and pinion unit in the low to mid torque range is a common fit for modulating butterfly valves.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Need an Actuator Package for Your Butterfly Valves?"
    subtitle="Send your valve size, seat material, working pressure and fail-safe requirement — we recommend the actuator and accessories within 24 hours. Email sales@pneumactuator.com for a factory-direct quote."
  />
</Layout>
